{"chunk_id": "7d7ce9302ea4", "text": "After a year of flawless service to a visiting ascetic, the maiden Pritha (Kunti) earns his deep satisfaction. When she refuses personal boons out of humility, the sage instead grants her a powerful Atharvan mantra that can summon any celestial being and compel that deity to obey her. Curious about its power, the young and unmarried Kunti later tests it at dawn by invoking Surya, the Sun god. Surya appears in radiant form, divided so that the sun continues shining while his divine presence stands before her. Alarmed, Kunti asks him to return, admitting she called him only out of curiosity. Surya refuses to leave without purpose, insisting she accept a son from him and threatening a curse if she refuses. The scene captures the dangerous power of divine gifts and sets in motion the fateful conception of Karna, one of the epic\u2019s most tragic heroes.\nCurious about Durvasa\u2019s mantra, the unmarried Kunti invokes the Sun god, who appears and insists she grant him a son.\nThemes: divine invocation, curiosity and consequence, maidenly honor, divine-human tension, birth of a hero", "vector": [0.008682680316269398, -0.019683517515659332, -0.004691839683800936, 0.0005942881689406931, -0.011250698938965797, 0.031260423362255096, -0.017601341009140015, -0.00032360508339479566, -0.0317046232521534, -0.02028040774166584, 0.014603004790842533, 0.016601895913481712, 0.012659639120101929, 0.02311217039823532, 0.0023702119942754507, 0.0030920335557311773, 0.04583566635847092, -0.02189062535762787, 0.01265269797295332, -0.03278735280036926, -0.009008888155221939, -0.011854530312120914, -0.013138540089130402, 0.007884511724114418, -0.0220988430082798, 0.01111188717186451, 0.013617441058158875, -0.03134371340274811, 0.011792064644396305, -0.028789574280381203, -0.006135482806712389, 0.0022227244917303324, -0.004945171531289816, -0.02233482338488102, -0.029761256650090218, 0.01008467935025692, 0.004712661728262901, -0.011986401863396168, 0.007